The state version and Target B route did not change between its stated base
`60e0d60801e1abfa2cfba65ba82ce89740c399f0` and this audit base.

### Theorem 1: bounded-displacement affine chart

The source candidate assumes actual global minimizers \(A_j\), diameters
\(D_j\to\infty\), and an injective matching of every source point to a point
of one unit oblique lattice \(\Lambda_{\theta_j}\), with uniformly bounded
displacement \(\varepsilon_j\). It proves

\[
b_{\Lambda_{\theta_j}}(K_j)=o(D_j),\qquad
D_j(\sin\theta_j-a_0)\le 4a_0\varepsilon_j+o(1),
\]

where \(K_j\) is the convex hull of the matched lattice points. In the exact
carrier case \(\varepsilon_j=0\), it obtains
\(D_j(\sin\theta_j-a_0)\to0\) and the isodiametric area deficit
\(\pi D_j^2/4-\operatorname{area}(K_j)=o(D_j)\).

The packing estimate is valid: centered fundamental cells of radius
\(\sqrt3/2\) around the distinct matched sites are disjoint and lie in
\(K_j+\mathbb B(0,\sqrt3/2)\). With
\(\operatorname{diam}K_j\le D_j+2\varepsilon_j\), Steiner's formula gives

\[
n_ja_j\le \operatorname{area}(K_j)
 +\frac{\pi\sqrt3}{2}(D_j+2\varepsilon_j)+\frac{3\pi}{4},
\quad a_j=\sin\theta_j.
\tag{2}
\]

The canonical strict translated-triangular-lattice comparison in Target B
Section 36 is

\[
n_j-1>\frac{\pi D_j^2}{4a_0}.
\tag{3}
\]

Since \(a_j\ge a_0\), (2)--(3) put \(K_j\), after scaling by \(D_j^{-1}\),
at isodiametric equality in the limit. Every Hausdorff limit is the radius
\(1/2\) disk. Therefore every edge of \(K_j\) is \(o(D_j)\).

For the boundary count, fix \(M>0\). There are uniformly finitely many
primitive lattice directions of length below \(M\), since
\(|iu+jv|^2\ge(i^2+j^2)/2\) throughout the angle interval. A convex polygon
has at most two edges in each such direction; these edges contribute
\(o(D_j)\) lattice boundary sites. The longer primitive directions contribute
at most \(\operatorname{per}(K_j)/M\). First taking \(j\to\infty\), then
\(M\to\infty\), proves \(b_{\Lambda}(K_j)=o(D_j)\). This argument is uniform
over the allowed changing angles.

Pick's identity on \(\Lambda\) is

\[
|\Lambda\cap K|=\frac{\operatorname{area}(K)}a+
\frac{b_\Lambda(K)}2+1.
\]

The candidate supplies a valid proof by triangulating with all lattice sites;
an empty lattice triangle has determinant one, and Euler's formula gives
Pick's count. Combining Pick, isodiametry, and (3) gives the finite bound

\[
D(a-a_0)<4a_0\varepsilon+
\frac{4a_0\varepsilon^2}{D}+
\frac{2aa_0}{\pi}\frac{b_\Lambda(K)}D.
\tag{4}
\]

The right side proves the claimed shear estimate. For \(\varepsilon=0\),
Pick and (3) also give
\(\operatorname{area}(K)>\pi D^2/4-a b_\Lambda(K)/2\), proving the stated
\(o(D)\) deficit.

I found no missing source point: the matching is injective and is indexed by
every actual point of \(A_j\), including any contact-isolated points.

### Theorem 2: pure-disk Pick ledger

For the candidate's exact one-disk rhombic complex, let \(R\) be the number of
rhombi, \(B\) its boundary edge count, and
\(M=|\Lambda\cap K|-|A|\). Euler's formula for a topological disk gives
\(n=R+B/2+1\), and Pick gives

\[
G=a\left(M+\frac{B-b_\Lambda(K)}2\right).
\]

Using \(E=(a-a_0)R\), these identities yield exactly

\[
G+E-\frac{a_0B}{2}
=(a-a_0)(n-1)+aM-\frac a2b_\Lambda(K).
\tag{5}
\]

The canonical strict pure-disk shell window is \(G+E<a_0B/2\) (Target B
Sections 36 and 38.1). Thus (5) implies

\[
(a-a_0)(n-1)+aM<\frac a2b_\Lambda(K).
\]

Together with Theorem 1, this gives \(M=o(D)\),
\(D(a-a_0)\to0\), and a shell gap \(a_0B/2-(G+E)=o(D)\). The algebra is
correct. The topology assumption is essential: the identity is not asserted
for multiple grains, holes, exceptional faces, or extra source points outside
the rhombic complex.

### Regression audit and status

The square-coordinate lattice box in the candidate is an exact regression
against any Pick-only shell contradiction. With
\(A_m=\{iu+jv:0\le i,j\le m\}\) and
\(\theta=\pi/3+m^{-2}\), it is unit-separated and triangle-free, with
\(R=m^2\), \(B=b_\Lambda(K)=4m\), \(G=M=0\), and
\(E=m^2(\sin\theta-a_0)<a_0B/2\) for \(m\ge2\). Its diameter is
\(\sim\sqrt3m\), so the strict triangular-lattice comparison shows it is far
from globally optimal. It falsifies a scalar implication only; it is not a
Level I counterexample.

The candidate's second regression is also valid: for each fixed \(n\), a
strict triangular-lattice feasible bound persists under a sufficiently small
increase of the basis angle by continuity, and the open oblique angle removes
unit equilateral triangles. This proves that the scalar diameter bound alone
does not characterize global minimizers.

Theorem 1 strengthens Target B Section 48.1 only when the affine carrier is a
unit oblique lattice with angle in \([\pi/3,2\pi/3]\) and the source-to-lattice
displacement is uniformly bounded. It supplies the additional sublinear
boundary count; it does not produce a global affine chart from the canonical
almost-rhombic core. Theorem 2 is an exact restricted ledger. Both claims
survive this independent proof and scope audit as **audited-working** results;
neither is canonical or a Target B closure.

## 4. Regressions, failed closures, and frontier effect

| Claim or route | Classification | Exact reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The same-diameter stretch under one diameter direction | Proved conditional theorem | Its hypotheses are exact and its conclusion is a global minimizer only when the input is one. An unbounded family is unknown. |
| Bounded-displacement lattice boundary count and shear estimate | Audited-working | Complete under the candidate's matching and unit oblique lattice hypotheses; no global chart follows from Target B. |
| Pure-disk Pick ledger | Audited-working | Exact for one edge-to-edge topological rhombic disk; no multi-grain or exceptional-face extension. |
| Universal Pick-only shell inequality | Rejected | The oblique lattice box above satisfies the strict shell window while remaining far from optimal. |
| Scalar strict diameter bound implies triangle-free global minimality | Rejected | The bound also holds for triangle-free feasible perturbations that are not known to be optimal. |
| Target B closure from this audit | Open | The multi-grain/global crossing and interface coupling is untouched. |
| Level I from this audit | Open | The conditional stretch route needs infinitely many global minimizers with one diameter direction; none are established. |

The affine route has no contradiction because the only remaining positive
terms are \((a-a_0)(n-1)+aM=o(D)\), matched by the sublinear hull-boundary
term. The shell inequality itself is strict only at that same sublinear scale.
No strict smaller-\(D\) source-preserving competitor was constructed.

The exact disproof obligation from the new stretch theorem is:

> Prove that for unboundedly many cardinalities, some global minimizer has all
> diameter pairs parallel; or replace this one-direction condition by a
> verifiable family of nonzero affine deformations that preserves every unit
> separation, keeps at least one diameter pair fixed, and makes every unit
> triangle non-equilateral.

If this fails, the positive route must at least rule out that exact flat
diameter mode before proving that a triangle in one minimizer is forced in
every minimizer.

